{"author":"RebarBotNava","author_email":"joeoakhart+bot@navapbc.com","author_id":"594c-9dcf-5ad6-4e6d","author_sig":"{\"payload\": \"\", \"payloadType\": \"application/vnd.rebar.authorship.v1+json\", \"signatures\": [{\"keyid\": \"594c-9dcf-5ad6-4e6d\", \"sig\": \"\"}]}","data":{"fields":{"description":"## Context / Problem\n\n`reconcile-check` still packages several distinct observables together: field-diff discrepancies, orphaned bindings, unbound counts, summary tallies, text formatting, exit behavior, and the persisted report. The legacy follow-on removals in `82bb` and `c195` both depend on first proving which of those observations belong to `bridge preview`, `bridge fsck`, or `bridge status`, and whether any observable truly lacks a home among those three existing owners.\n\n## Approach\n\n1. Add RED-first characterization coverage that captures the current `reconcile-check` contract for healthy, discrepant, orphaned, and unbound cases, including the fact that a successful run exits 0 regardless of findings and only unexpected failures exit 1.\n2. Build an observable-to-owner matrix that assigns each current `reconcile-check` observation to an existing canonical bridge owner:\n   - `bridge preview`: field-diff discrepancies and their preview-oriented formatting\n   - `bridge fsck`: orphaned bindings, orphaned Jira references, unbound local/Jira counts, and integrity-oriented formatting\n   - `bridge status`: summary tallies, operational witnesses, and the migrated persisted-report location or status payload metadata\n   - exit behavior: no new aggregate exit is introduced; each canonical owner keeps its pre-existing native exit contract, and the migration documentation records that `reconcile-check` itself did not have a findings-driven exit signal to preserve\n3. Implement the bridge-side routing needed to expose those preserved observables through the canonical owners. Introduce a new bridge diagnostic subcommand only if the observable-to-owner matrix proves that a named current observable cannot be expressed through preview, fsck, or status; if that happens, record the forcing observable and rationale in the ticket comment and migration doc.\n4. Migrate persisted report construction away from `bridge_state/reconcile-check.json` only to the extent required by the owner remap, and record every retired field or output in both the migration doc and a ticket comment with its name, rationale, replacement owner (or explicit absence), and the characterization assertion that proves the old surface is gone.\n\n## Scope\n\n- direct `reconcile-check` report construction and output shaping in `src/rebar/_engine/rebar_reconciler`\n- canonical `bridge preview` / `bridge fsck` / `bridge status` CLI result contracts in `src/rebar/_cli`\n- bridge diagnostics surfaced through library/MCP shims only where needed to preserve the canonical bridge result contract before `c195` removes compatibility entrypoints\n- migration docs and characterization tests covering healthy, discrepant, orphaned, and unbound cases\n\n## Testing\n\nWrite RED-first characterization tests for the current `reconcile-check` report fields, formatting, persisted output, and exit-0-on-findings behavior. After the migration, prove that preview, fsck, and status expose the preserved diagnostics at their assigned owners; that any retired field/output is absent with explicit migration evidence; and that each owner retains its native exit contract without inventing a new aggregate reconcile-check exit.\n\n## Acceptance Criteria\n\n- [ ] Every current `reconcile-check` observable is assigned to `bridge preview`, `bridge fsck`, or `bridge status`; if any observable is retired instead, the migration doc and ticket comment name the retired observable, rationale, replacement owner or explicit absence, and the characterization assertion that proves the retirement.\n- [ ] `bridge preview` exposes field-diff discrepancies and preview-specific formatting under its existing result contract.\n- [ ] `bridge fsck` exposes orphaned-binding, orphaned-Jira, unbound-count, and integrity-formatting diagnostics under its existing result contract.\n- [ ] `bridge status` exposes summary tallies, operational witnesses, and the migrated persisted-report location or metadata under stable names.\n- [ ] The migration preserves the source fact that `reconcile-check` had no findings-driven exit signal: preview/fsck/status keep their existing native exits, and the migration doc plus characterization coverage record that there is no new aggregate exit parity contract to preserve.\n- [ ] A new bridge diagnostic subcommand is introduced only if the observable-to-owner matrix proves a named current observable has no home in preview/fsck/status, and the ticket comment records that forcing case.\n- [ ] Characterization and canonical bridge tests cover healthy, discrepant, orphaned, and unbound cases."}},"env_id":"b8a354e0-12c3-4cf2-bbf1-fcc9685d5126","event_type":"EDIT","timestamp":1788426052044067001,"uuid":"50eadcfd-0744-4f83-9372-eb79b5a27e49"}